1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 { The King's Indian: Black lets White build a centre and strikes back later with ...e5 or ...c5. }
3. Nc3
( 3. Nf3 { [%name Fianchetto Variation] }
  3... Bg7 4. g3 O-O 5. Bg2 d6 6. O-O Nbd7 7. Nc3 e5 8. e4 c6 { Black keeps a flexible centre and prepares ...Qb6 or ...a5. }
  9. h3 )
3... Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 { [%name Classical, Mar del Plata] }
( 5. f3 { [%name Sämisch] White secures e4 and prepares Be3, Qd2 and a kingside attack. }
  5... O-O 6. Be3 e5 7. d5 Nh5 { The knight eyes f4 and prepares ...f5. }
  8. Qd2 f5 { Black fights for the light squares on the kingside. } )
( 5. f4 { [%name Four Pawns Attack] The most ambitious setup: four pawns in the centre. }
  5... O-O 6. Nf3 c5 { Hitting the centre before it becomes overwhelming. }
  7. d5 e6 8. Be2 exd5 9. cxd5 Bg4 10. O-O Nbd7 { A Benoni structure; Black aims at e5 and the queenside majority. } )
5... O-O 6. Be2 e5 { The thematic central claim. }
7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 { The knight heads to the kingside to support ...f5. }
9. Ne1 Nd7 10. Nd3 f5 11. Bd2 Nf6 12. f3 f4 { The Mar del Plata race: Black storms the kingside, White breaks with c5 on the queenside. } *
